#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Step 05 Deal and push: both sides seal halves of one deal and push them; each holds and cites the other's.

Usage: 05-deal --population DIR --out DIR

The rehearsal population's `alice` and `bob` (their TEST keys) make one deal,
each in its own book, through the driver steps/05-deal.setup.sh installs (a
Rust build of evidencebook's crate at the pinned tag):

- each side opens its book and cuts a checkpoint, which the other side learns;
- each side seals its half of the deal (the deal id, the exchange's request
  and response digests, the other side's key) citing the other side's
  checkpoint, and appends it under a new checkpoint;
- at completion each side whose `share_record_at_completion` is
  `counterparty` pushes its half as a record-push bundle (the half, its
  inclusion proof, the checkpoint it is under);
- the receiver checks the push, holds the bytes as transmitted by their
  record id, and seals a record in its own book citing the other half
  (`citation_purpose: counterparty_half`) and the checkpoint it came under.

Each node's setting is `<population dir>/step05/settings.json`
(`{"<node>": {"share_record_at_completion": "counterparty" | "off"}}`); a node
it does not name is `off`, the default of the pinned capsules plugin.

The proof bytes (deal.json, verify.json and, for each side, its open
checkpoint, half, push, held stores, citing record, checkpoints and proofs)
are then checked by the driver's `verify`, with evidencebook's library: record
ids recomputed, seals, checkpoint signatures and signers, inclusion. Its
verdict is verify.json.

The receiver records how it resolved each push's sender, `accepted_via`
(the shape the pinned capsules plugin records, capsules-plugin PR #77):
`controller_declaration` when a controller declaration binds the sender's
evidence key, else `operator_stopgap`; a node set to `host_announced` records
that. The declarations come from `<population dir>/step05/controllers.json`,
each signed by its controller's TEST key; their format is the rehearsal's
stand-in, not the controller-binding/0 the plugin reads. Refusals are replies
that name the refused key_id and are not sealed.

The finding:
- FAIL when any check on the proof bytes does not hold (a half citing the
  wrong counterparty checkpoint, a held half whose bytes do not recompute to
  the digest it is held and cited by, a side that pushed nothing, a
  counterparty whose key is bound to the receiver's own controller).
- PASS when every check held and each side accepted under a controller
  declaration that verifies and binds the sender's key.
- NOT_CHECKED when every check held but a side accepted via
  `operator_stopgap` or `host_announced`: acceptance is then the one
  not_checked entry (ACCEPTANCE_ISSUE), never a pass.

On the live population each lab node's capsules plugin data dir is read
(steps/live.py): the halves it holds from its counterparties
(`ledger/received-capsules.jsonl`, with their provenance and the bundle's
checkpoint and proof) and its own chain (`ledger/capsules.jsonl`). For each
held half: the AAC Go verifier (step 01's pin) runs Class 1 on the held bytes
and recomputes the capsule_id from them; the driver checks the half is
included under the checkpoint it was pushed with, signed by the half's key;
and the node's own chain carries a record citing it as `counterparty_half`,
whose capsule_id recomputes. For a half sealed under the current build only, the chain must also carry a
`counterparty_inclusion` record that recomputes and cites the checkpoint it was pushed under (the digest the
driver recomputes from the held checkpoint). That citing record carries how the node accepted the half (`received_half.accepted_via` and
`sender_key_id`, capsules-plugin PR #77): a record naming a key other than the half's, an unknown
`accepted_via`, or a declaration with no binding digest or anchored on the node's own key (a
declaration under a bip340 anchor has no anchor key, so that check applies only when one is present),
or `received_half.same_controller_as_receiver` true (capsules-plugin#135), is a FAIL. A citing record
naming a `controller_binding_digest` equal to the `controller_binding.digest` in the sender's
published commitment (`nodes/commitments/<node_id>.json` in this checkout, loop#88; the sender is
the node whose `nodes/<node_id>.json` lists the half's key) counts as accepted under a controller
declaration however the plugin first accepted the key, leaving only the binding's own signatures
(AAC#432) unchecked.

The tie (loop#107): each node seals a record naming both halves of an exchange (the citing record's
`received_half.own_half_capsule_id`, capsules-plugin#138) and cites the other side's checkpoint. It must
name an own half in the node's chain, of the same `client_nonce` and the opposite role, whose bytes
verify (else FAIL). A citing record without it is "held, never tied", its own not_checked entry; a nonce
that now has more than one own half or another held half makes the tie ambiguous at verify time (it is
sealed at seal time only), another entry, never a pass. A node's `sharing-effective.json`
(capsules-plugin#137) is read and labelled "node-reported, unsigned"; the push is still judged from
the sealed records.

Only halves sealed under the current build are judged for acceptance and the tie: LOOP_SEALED_SINCE
(RFC 3339, the instant the lab's current build started) is compared to the citing record's timestamp,
and earlier halves are counted and still checked for their bytes, provenance, held inclusion proof and citing
record (not for the counterparty_inclusion record, the tie or acceptance).
Unset, every held half is judged. Current-build halves accepted on the evidence key from a sender
whose published commitment carries no equal operator binding are their own entry ("halves from an
unbound sender"), not a plugin gap. The other parts the pinned plugin does not do, or the loop cannot
read, are not_checked entries: the declaration's own signatures (AAC#432) and the held half's
signature in a second language (AAC#431). A node with no held half, or whose data_dir is not readable,
is NOT_CHECKED. One finding, the worst over the nodes.

No spend: this step starts no exchange and pays nothing on either population.
On the live population it reads the halves the lab run's exchanges left; the
spend of those exchanges is the lab run's, inside its cap, and is recorded
there. The finding's detail says so.

Exit 0 once the finding is written, whatever the result; non-zero when the
step itself broke (no driver installed, unreadable population).
"""
